# Quality gates and release evidence

The gate verifies outcomes, not just process exit codes. A successful compiler,
high line coverage, or a browser mock is not proof of a correct native release.
Keep implementation plans and transient verification logs outside the repository.

## Change review

Prefer independently reviewable PRs with one feature or mechanism. Record the exact
tested commit, executed commands, failing-baseline evidence, native runner links,
and limitations in the PR. Follow the [validation stages](testing-feedback-loop.md#validation-stages-and-agent-responsibilities)
for affected feedback and boundary evidence; comprehensive local preparation is
optional, and current-candidate protected CI remains required before merge.
Use the existing [PR preparation entry point](testing-feedback-loop.md#fast-pr-creation-and-optional-full-local-preparation)
when comprehensive local evidence is needed. The full health gate builds frontend
assets before Tauri compilation, including on a fresh checkout.

[CI maintenance](ci-maintenance.md) owns efficiency and reliability decisions.
Gate scope, evidence requirements and release acceptance remain owned here;
an optimization proposal does not itself authorize reducing them.

For each applicable scenario, assert externally observable outcomes. A test should
fail with the old implementation or a deliberate incorrect mutation. Do not weaken
assertions to accommodate unexplained failures.

| Change                 | Required scenarios in addition to the happy path                                                                               |
| ---------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| Persistent state       | Reopen/restart, append after torn record, read/write failure, owner isolation, no memory-only success after failed persistence |
| Permissions/privacy    | Allow and deny, revocation after existing session, restart recovery, unknown peer/device, group vs direct-message boundaries   |
| Async UI/media         | Account switch/logout while request is pending, late response rejection, failed save and retry, media cleanup                  |
| Multiple devices/files | Every destination, one logical history record, sender restart before recipient download, revoked access                        |
| Platform/native UI     | Real Tauri IPC and window/process state on the affected OS, negative control, runner/desktop/GPU limitations                   |
| CI/release scripts     | Real fixture files, missing/partial evidence, tool failures, changed source and stale artifacts                                |

Rust unit/integration, real multi-process backend E2E, browser UI tests, and native
desktop tests answer different questions. A VM/Xvfb/software-renderer pass does not
establish that a reported physical GPU or VMware compositor fault is fixed.

## Test quality and evidence

`node --test scripts/diagnostics/*.test.mjs` runs in three-platform CI and the health
gate. Python 3.11+ is required for archive regressions; CI pins Python 3.12. The
diagnostic suite includes actual release-script execution with deliberate verifier
failures, real ZIP corruption fixtures, and release-state changes after verification.
Full health requires cargo-deny, cargo-machete, typos, Gitleaks, ShellCheck and
actionlint; missing tools fail rather than being silently skipped. CI installs them
explicitly. `--fast` remains a clearly labeled partial local check.

Health runs as a reusable workflow inside the UI orchestrator for every PR and
merge candidate, and independently on its weekly/manual triggers. All three
platform diagnostic suites remain mandatory. The complete frontend unit suite
runs once with coverage on Linux; its platform branches use explicit mocks,
while real browser/native behavior retains the platform matrices. Source-only
formatting, type/lint and dependency policy run once. The Linux coverage producer seals
its line map for same-run UI consumers; source, workflow, run, attempt and digest
must all match. Superseded PR and merge candidates are cancelled automatically. Every main push
has its own verification group, preserving pending commits for release evidence.
Health uses a distinct caller-workflow/event namespace, so independent manual or
scheduled runs cannot cancel the UI caller or its same-run evidence producer.

Branch protection requires direct execution checks instead of runner-backed
copies of their aggregate result. `scripts/ci/required-checks.mjs` owns the direct
release policy and replacement mapping. Source and all platform checks are
individually required; Windows requires its build producer and all archive
consumers. Release acceptance uses the same direct names plus its main-only
checks, including ASan and mutation-runner acceptance. A newer failed, pending or
missing check still rejects the exact source. Stable repeats this strict check
immediately before public container upload and final release publication, after
lengthy preparation; Nightly retains its final strict recheck. To inspect a migration, run
`node scripts/ci/migrate-required-checks.mjs OWNER/REPO main PR_NUMBER --plan`.
Use `--apply` only after the replacement checks pass on the current PR head. The
command validates each bound GitHub App, rejects a changed candidate/policy,
preserves strictness and unrelated checks, and verifies the readback. A missing
old context before migration is pending, never permission to bypass a gate.
Diagnostics run on the actual native test machine; a probe on another VM cannot
certify that machine's network capability. Windows gates explicitly use Bash;
opening a script through a file association does not establish execution.

Mutation testing uses pinned cargo-mutants 27.1.0 and examines all Rust workspace
members on daily and manual runs. PR, push and merge-queue runs validate the pinned
runner with real fixture crates; they do not sample product mutations. See the
[mutation execution policy](testing-feedback-loop.md) for ownership and budgets.
Each broad run rotates through one of 256 round-robin samples and partitions that
same sample across four runners (subshards of 1024), without changing the selected
mutant set. Each runner uses one in-place worker, incremental compilation, the
symbol-free `mutants` Cargo profile, and the workspace-root build cache. Every
mutant still runs the workspace tests through cargo-nextest pinned by the
[shared CI tool owner](../scripts/ci/windows-coverage.mjs). Its
`mutants` profile isolates each test in a process and fails tests exceeding 60
seconds (with a five-second termination grace period). This catches injected busy
loops even when they prevent an async timeout from being polled. The 360-second
whole-suite deadline remains a hard failure. Reports are uploaded as `mutants-report-0`
through `mutants-report-3`; assess all four reports for the complete sample.

The pinned mutation tool runs an unmutated baseline for the packages containing
the selected mutations; each mutation runs the complete workspace. Record the
actual commands and inventories, and retain the separate workspace CI gate. Empty selections
only validate selection/tooling and do not constitute baseline evidence. Tiny
dependency-free fixture crates check the actual pinned CLI, caught/advisory outcomes,
baseline failure, empty selection, and hanging mutations via
`node --test scripts/release/run-mutants.test.mjs`.
Selected counts, completed outcomes, baseline,
tool exit code and summary counts must agree. Zero selected mutations are recorded
explicitly and may have no report; a stale report is rejected.

Survivors remain advisory and must be triaged in review. Unviable mutants are not
caught mutants. Timeouts, usage/diff/internal errors, absent reports and incomplete
sweeps fail the job: they are inconclusive, not successful validation. The full sweep
still has a runtime budget; a timed-out sweep is not represented as complete.
Report formats and exit codes follow [cargo-mutants documentation](https://mutants.rs/mutants-out.html)
and [the pinned exit-code definitions](https://github.com/sourcefrog/cargo-mutants/blob/v27.1.0/src/exit_code.rs).

Coverage remains a signal, not a substitute for assertions or mutation review. Do
not count test-module lines as production-only coverage, unviable mutants as killed,
or skipped/inconclusive native scenarios as passed.

## Release workflow

Tag pushes and dispatches with `publish_release=false` build and verify a draft:

`preflight → 6 desktop target jobs → aggregate verification → draft upload → downloaded-draft verification`

A dispatch with `publish_release=true` reuses that verified build:

`preflight → resolve verified build → download evidence and draft → re-verify → current-source CI and container checks → final snapshot guard → publication`

Preflight rejects version/tag mismatches, tags not already merged into main and
published releases. Workflow runs for the same ref are serialized. A branch dry run
uses `v<version>-ci.<run-id>` archive names and never writes a GitHub release.

Each platform signs its ZIP and records build provenance. Builds only upload workflow
artifacts; they cannot independently publish or update a release. The aggregate job
requires exactly 12 ZIPs (six desktop target archives and six headless
archives), 12 signature bundles and one nonempty CycloneDX SBOM: 25 release assets.
Native builds reuse the Tauri CLI installed by `npm ci` from the committed lockfile,
instead of compiling an unpinned latest CLI independently on every runner.

The gate verifies signatures against the exact repository, workflow and source ref,
and provenance against the exact source commit on GitHub-hosted runners. It checks
ZIP members before extracting individual installers for native inspection: duplicate
or unsafe members, symlinks, unexpected formats, blank/partial/duplicate/wrong
checksums and empty installers are rejected.
The macOS ZIP contains the DMG and checksum manifest; the app is inside the DMG.

Metadata inspection never executes or installs application binaries:

- macOS: inspect actual DMG UDIF structure and embedded app with 7-Zip; check its
  version and executable architecture.
- Windows: inspect PE version and bootstrapper architecture, MSI ProductVersion and
  target-specific x64/Arm64 Template. An x86 NSIS bootstrapper installing a native app is valid.
- Linux: inspect Debian/RPM version and architecture, AppImage format and embedded ELF.
  Use `unsquashfs -cat` at the runtime's validated ELF-derived filesystem offset,
  without executing AppImage; 7-Zip codec support differs across operating systems.
  The offset calculation follows the [official Type 2 runtime](https://github.com/AppImage/type2-runtime/blob/main/src/runtime/runtime.c).
  AppImage has no application-version metadata; bind its `.text` and `.rodata` to the
  versioned Debian executable, normalizing only Tauri's fixed-width bundle-type markers.
  Any other code/constants difference fails.

This is artifact correctness verification, not proof that installation, OS trust
prompts, every desktop/GPU, or every application feature works. Native functional
tests remain necessary. The SBOM is checked for inventory and byte equality between
workflow and draft; it does not have a separate signature bundle.

Only after all workflow assets pass are they centrally uploaded to a draft. Draft
status is rechecked immediately before upload. The actual draft is re-downloaded and
re-verified; its bytes must equal workflow output and GitHub asset digests. Evidence
binds the repository, source SHA, release ID and each asset ID/size/digest.

Tag pushes keep releases as drafts. To explicitly publish a verified stable tag:

```sh
gh workflow run release.yml --ref vX.Y.Z -f publish_release=true
```

The publishing dispatch does not compile again. It resolves the latest eligible
successful build for the exact repository, source commit, stable tag and release
workflow, including successful downloaded-draft verification. A newer failed or
incomplete build blocks publication; an older success cannot mask it. Missing,
expired or ambiguous verification evidence also blocks publication.

The dispatch downloads that build's `verified-release-evidence` and container
artifacts, then downloads the current draft. It re-verifies signatures, provenance,
installer metadata and the recorded snapshot, including repository/source/tag,
release and asset IDs, sizes, digests and actual file bytes. It then requires
current-source CI, every direct Health check, UI/backend E2E, Gitleaks, Scorecard, all
three CodeQL languages and no unresolved main code-scanning alerts, plus container
smoke tests before publishing relay images. Missing, running, skipped or failed
checks stop publication. The latest check run is used; an older success cannot mask
a newer failure.

Immediately before publication, the gate rechecks draft state and all bound asset
identities/digests, then publishes by the verified release ID, not just a mutable tag
lookup. If checks are pending, wait and rerun the publishing dispatch after they
complete; no duplicate compilation or manual cancellation is needed. Do not manually
publish, edit assets or move tags while a release workflow is active: GitHub does not
provide one atomic transaction covering asset writes and publication, and workflow
concurrency cannot lock external administrator actions. Published versions are never
rebuilt or overwritten by this workflow; use a new version for corrections.

The `verified-release-evidence` workflow artifact and job summary retain source and
asset hashes. A failed build, verifier or metadata tool leaves the release unpublished.

To run the gate locally (requires cosign, gh, Python 3.11+, exiftool, msiinfo, dpkg-deb,
rpm, 7zz/7z and unsquashfs from `squashfs-tools`; the directory must contain only the 37 release assets):

```sh
bash scripts/release/verify-release.sh /path/to/assets vX.Y.Z X.Y.Z \
  OctopusGarage/mesh-talk refs/tags/vX.Y.Z <exact-source-commit>
```
# AI coding reliability guide

Use this guide before implementing or reviewing a change, especially a bug fix,
refactor, merge resolution or optimization. Its purpose is to turn recurring
failure modes into concrete design questions and executable checks. Compiling,
adding a timeout or passing a happy-path test does not establish reliability.

This guide owns the cross-cutting review method and reusable failure patterns.
[Module ownership](module-boundaries.md) owns implementation boundaries;
[architecture](ARCHITECTURE.md), [network contracts](network-design-reference.md)
and feature guides own behavior and resource limits. The
[testing workflow](testing-feedback-loop.md) owns commands, selection and gates.
Link to those owners instead of copying their changing contracts here. When code
and a contract disagree, resolve the intended behavior rather than documenting
the regression as the new rule.

## Use on every change

1. **Locate the owner and consumers.** Read the owning implementation, callers,
   existing tests and relevant guide before adding another helper or state owner.
   Include callbacks, IPC handlers, retries, startup, shutdown and recovery paths;
   an import graph alone cannot reveal all consumers.
2. **State the invariant and select risks.** Describe what must remain true on
   success, failure, cancellation and replacement. Use the table below to select
   applicable rules. A small diff can change lock order or lifetime semantics.
3. **Trace the failure path before editing.** For concurrency work, sketch who
   owns each lock, permit, task and mutable state; what it waits for; and who
   releases it. For a reproducible bug, construct a focused failing regression.
   Record unresolved hypotheses as hypotheses, not confirmed defects.
4. **Implement at the existing boundary.** Preserve authority, durable acceptance,
   compatibility and resource budgets. Recheck invariants after moving work
   outside a guard. Evaluate the proposed fix for new races and deadlocks too.
5. **Verify the changed boundary and report evidence.** Run affected checks and
   required boundary scenarios under the testing workflow. Explain results,
   exclusions and unresolved evidence. Update this guide when the defect exposes
   a reusable failure pattern; follow the maintenance procedure below.

Keep the risk explanation proportional to the change. A documentation edit does
not require a new concurrency test. A changed async callback does require a
lifetime review even when the diff is one line. Put task-specific reasoning and
results in the PR or ignored local evidence, not a new permanent report.

## Select rules by the changed boundary

| Change or symptom                                                        | Read at least                                                                                                                                         |
| ------------------------------------------------------------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Locks, callbacks, synchronous dispatch, UI freezes                       | [R01](#r01-lock-order-and-reentrant-calls), [R02](#r02-thread-and-scheduler-progress), [R10](#r10-evidence-that-exercises-the-failure)                |
| Background work, native providers, logout, shutdown or cancellation      | [R02](#r02-thread-and-scheduler-progress), [R03](#r03-actual-work-owns-its-lifetime), [R06](#r06-exact-authority-and-stale-completions)               |
| Network requests, retries, polling, queues or batches                    | [R03](#r03-actual-work-owns-its-lifetime), [R04](#r04-deadlines-cover-the-whole-operation), [R05](#r05-bounded-work-must-also-make-progress)          |
| React effects, IPC reads, dialogs, optimistic updates or mutation status | [R06](#r06-exact-authority-and-stale-completions), [R07](#r07-observation-is-not-mutation-ownership), [R10](#r10-evidence-that-exercises-the-failure) |
| Storage, settings, imports, deletion or recovery                         | [R06](#r06-exact-authority-and-stale-completions), [R08](#r08-durable-state-and-failure-semantics), [R09](#r09-bound-input-and-resource-use)          |
| IPC/API inputs, files, media or protocol changes                         | [R06](#r06-exact-authority-and-stale-completions), [R08](#r08-durable-state-and-failure-semantics), [R09](#r09-bound-input-and-resource-use)          |
| Merge resolution, shared helper or dependency changes                    | Revisit the affected callers and all applicable rules; a clean textual merge does not establish compatible ownership or semantics.                    |

These are starting points, not permission to ignore another reachable boundary.
Do not add a global lock, retry loop, timeout or shared abstraction solely to make
the local symptom disappear.

A frozen UI may result from a lock cycle, blocked event thread, starvation, lost
wakeup, unbounded work or stale loading state. Identify the mechanism and the
missing progress condition before selecting a fix; these need different evidence.

## R01: Lock order and reentrant calls

**Failure pattern:** one path takes A then B while another takes B then A; or a
helper/callback reacquires a nonrecursive lock its caller already holds. The
second case can deadlock on a single thread. Nested reads also need review when
a queued writer can prevent a new read from completing.

Trace actual acquisition order through callees, authorization callbacks,
destructors and error cleanup. Include main-thread dispatch and task joins as
wait dependencies, not just mutexes. A typical UI cycle is:

```text
background task: holds session guard -> waits for main-thread callback
main thread:     handles UI event    -> waits for session guard
```

Use the owning module's order; do not invent one universal order for unrelated
subsystems. Make whether a helper acquires a guard or requires one explicit.
Avoid invoking arbitrary callbacks while guarded. Where a commit callback must
run inside an authority guard, keep that contract narrow and inspect its complete
call chain. Queue presentation without retaining a guard needed by the UI;
validate captured authority when the queued callback executes.

**Evidence:** exercise both competing paths and the real authorization variant.
An ordinary admission fixture does not cover a sensitive proof that itself locks
app state. Assert that the other operation progresses, not just that the first
eventually returns. See `checked_at_lock_revision` in
[runtime admission](../src-tauri/src/runtime_admission.rs), the verified-login
regression in [shared login tests](../src-tauri/src/shared_account_login/tests.rs),
and queued notification tests in [runtime state](../src-tauri/src/chat_commands/state.rs).

## R02: Thread and scheduler progress

**Failure pattern:** an `async` function performs blocking disk, crypto, OS-provider
or process work; a blocking worker retains a globally needed guard; or the parent
scheduler awaits optional work before polling unrelated ready operations.

Identify the executing thread and scheduling owner, not just the function's
# Keeping CI fast and reliable

Treat feedback time, reliability and resource use as maintained requirements.
Every feature, dependency and test can change CI cost, even without a workflow
edit. AI agents and contributors should preserve useful coverage while reducing
unnecessary work, and leave failures easier to reproduce and diagnose.

This guide owns CI maintenance decisions and regression prevention. It does not
introduce another workflow, command catalog or acceptance policy.

| Question                                            | Authoritative owner                                                                                                                     |
| --------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Which tests, commands and CI jobs apply?            | [Testing feedback loop](testing-feedback-loop.md), owning `validation.json` contracts and [workflow definitions](../.github/workflows/) |
| What evidence permits acceptance or release?        | [Quality gates](quality-gates.md) and [version and release lifecycle](updates.md#version-ownership-and-lifecycle)                       |
| Where does an implementation or shared rule belong? | [Module ownership](module-boundaries.md) and [script entry points](../scripts/README.md)                                                |
| Which consumers and documentation need updating?    | [Change alignment checklist](maintenance-checklist.md) and [documentation guidelines](DOCUMENTATION_GUIDELINES.md)                      |

Keep tool versions, matrix sizes, deadlines, cache keys and required-check names
in their existing executable owners. Keep run histories and investigation output
in ignored `tmp/` or PR/CI artifacts; durable decisions belong in the owning guide.

## Make the cost of a change explicit

Before adding a suite, fixture, dependency, platform or workflow, identify the
existing owner and the observable failure it must detect. Inspect the affected
plan and consumers through the [validation protocol](testing-feedback-loop.md#validation-stages-and-agent-responsibilities).
Answer four questions in the change when they are material:

- What requirement needs new evidence, and why is existing evidence insufficient?
- Which layer and platform can exercise that requirement faithfully?
- What recurring setup, compilation, execution and artifact cost will it add?
- Can an existing task, fixture or compatible output supply it without weakening
  assertions or introducing another owner for the same rule?

Extend the existing implementation first. Share policy and lifecycle rules where
their meaning is identical; keep feature-specific fixtures local. An abstraction
is useful when it removes duplicated decisions and has clear inputs, outputs and
failure behavior. Avoid both copied shell fragments and a generic CI framework
whose configuration is harder to review than the work it coordinates.

Do not make a full local suite the default for every edit. Use affected feedback
while developing, then complete the applicable boundary and candidate gates.
New tests still need independent assertions; matching the implementation or
increasing a test count does not establish useful coverage.

## Keep workflow policy thin and reject cheap failures locally

Workflows own triggers, permissions, runners, matrices, dependencies and artifact
transport. Scripts own inventory validation, artifact roles, packaging, report
collection and execution policy. Reuse the same CLI locally and in Actions;
extract substantial conditionals and loops into the existing feature owner rather
than copying YAML fragments or introducing another generic orchestration layer.
When moving execution into a script, update every caller and smoke check. Keep
launcher behavior in its owning regression instead of requiring obsolete command
text in YAML. Cross-platform filesystem paths use `fileURLToPath` rather than
`.pathname`; ESM module arguments retain a file URL or relative module specifier
instead of a native Windows absolute path. Exercise each boundary on its platform.
Keep live source/release checks immediately before each publication boundary;
those protect against changed state and are not redundant setup checks.

The [syntax owner](../scripts/validation/syntax.mjs) parses changed Node, Bash and
Python sources without executing them or writing Python caches, and invokes
`actionlint` for changed workflows. Pre-commit selects staged paths but checks
worktree bytes, matching the existing structural checks. Affected validation also
checks new nonignored sources; PR creation checks the clean committed comparison.
Use Node 22, Bash and Python; install actionlint before changing workflows. No
checker installs tools or contacts the network. Hook installation configures hooks
without launching comprehensive health; request that gate explicitly.

PR creation runs `make pr-check`'s cheap-check owner against its actual comparison
base, rejects ownership blockers and checks applicable frontend patch coverage
before publication. Version projections, contract references and hygiene already
belong to structural standards; reuse that owner rather than adding duplicate
gates. Do not install dependencies, compile Rust, launch browsers or run application suites
implicitly in the cheap mode. Source changes during checking invalidate it.
Workflow edits also run the existing fast CI policy owner locally. Configuration
assertions and runner inventory discovery belong here; application execution and
native compiler fixtures do not. Update the existing policy assertion when its
requirement changes instead of adding a second overlapping test in another file.
For entry-point changes, also run the affected contract tests selected by the
existing validation plan before publication. A syntax pass or a hand-picked
filename sample cannot detect stale semantic routing expectations; cheap PR
checks do not certify those tests passed.

The frontend precheck reads the target from `codecov.yml`, includes partially
covered branches in the uncovered denominator and rejects omitted changed files.
Exercise report readers against actual producer output as well as small fixtures;
negative V8 branch counts remain uncovered rather than invalidating the report.
It verifies an existing clean-candidate `frontend-coverage` receipt through the
canonical stage owner and rechecks LCOV bytes, rather than trusting a timestamp or
raw report. Generate missing evidence explicitly with the reported command. A
verification-only miss never starts coverage, waits for a heavy lock or claims a
pass; a no-work verification lease must not imply admitted child work. Keep
verified scope termination diagnostics separate from the actual test result.
This frontend unit precheck is conservative; Rust and browser-union coverage
still need their owning evidence. Native execution, dependency resolution and
published-asset verification cannot be replaced by parsing or stale build output.

Use the existing package inventory's `frontendBuild` ownership when preparing
Rust selectors. Core, integrations and MCP selections still require real target

