553:        "Functional browser case timeouts are process-safety watchdogs for complete cold/render/interaction journeys, not per-action performance SLAs. Full Chromium uses its established Windows/coverage allowance on all platforms; portable cases retain their existing Windows allowance and the full-suite allowance elsewhere. Keep all individual assertion and explicit product/latency deadlines, zero retries, full inventories and first-failure traces. The real runner control derives both actual source watchdogs, accelerates only their duration, requires a parked case to fail plus a later case to run, and joins descendants before deleting fixtures. Full Chromium shards on Linux, macOS and Windows produce portable core evidence once, with exact platform-specific source/run/attempt/platform identity (eight macOS shards, four Linux/Windows shards) and complete authoritative core inventory. Revalidate PNGs, traces and scenario identity on collection, rejecting missing, duplicate, skipped or flaky cases. Portable specs must explicitly retain successful traces under the full-suite configuration; verify changed scenarios with that configuration and evidence extraction as well as standalone portable execution, whose global trace setting can mask an omitted spec-level declaration. Dedicated WebKit and Firefox core acceptance are required on ordinary candidates; scheduled/manual extended Chromium and Firefox retain complete extended inventories. Fixture navigation must observe an actual boundary using browser-supported input rather than assume a wheel delta equals a scroll offset; retain genuine virtualized unmount, cache reuse and geometry assertions. Browser-side error evidence preserves bounded stacks and nested causes before protocol serialization, across navigation and disposal, while the unchanged strict error gate rejects uncaught/recovered errors. Validate early error/rejection, cycle/depth/string/event limits and the real-browser negative gate control. Standalone Windows portable-runner batches derive from complete inventory and remain at most 20 scenarios; run actual hosted Windows full shards to establish timing and acceptance. See docs/testing-feedback-loop.md."
name: Tauri Release

on:
  schedule:
    - cron: "23 3 * * *"
  push:
    tags:
      - "v*" # Triggered when pushing tags
  workflow_dispatch:
    inputs:
      publish_nightly:
        description: Build and publish a verified Nightly prerelease from main
        type: boolean
        default: false
      publish_release:
        description: Publish the verified draft (select a version tag, not a branch)
        type: boolean
        default: false

concurrency:
  group: release-${{ (github.event_name == 'schedule' || inputs.publish_nightly) && 'nightly' || github.ref }}
  cancel-in-progress: false

permissions:
  contents: read

env:
  CARGO_TERM_COLOR: always

jobs:
  preflight:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    timeout-minutes: 250
    permissions:
      actions: read
      contents: read
      checks: read
      security-events: read
    outputs:
      nightly: ${{ steps.inputs.outputs.nightly }}
      skip: ${{ steps.inputs.outputs.skip }}
      tag: ${{ steps.inputs.outputs.tag }}
      version: ${{ steps.inputs.outputs.version }}
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v7.0.1
      - uses: actions/setup-node@v7.1.0
        with:
          node-version: 22
      - name: Validate source, version and draft state
        id: inputs
        env:
          GH_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN }}
          RELEASE_NIGHTLY: ${{ github.event_name == 'schedule' || inputs.publish_nightly }}
          RELEASE_PUBLICATION: ${{ github.event_name == 'workflow_dispatch' && inputs.publish_release }}
        run: node scripts/release/preflight.mjs
      - name: Wait for successful exact-source CI before Nightly compilation
        if: steps.inputs.outputs.nightly == 'true' && steps.inputs.outputs.skip != 'true'
        env:
          GH_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN }}
        run: node scripts/release/check-source-checks.mjs "$GITHUB_REPOSITORY" "$GITHUB_SHA" --wait

  build-release:
    needs: preflight
    if: inputs.publish_release != true && needs.preflight.outputs.skip != 'true'
    permissions:
      contents: read
      id-token: write
      attestations: write
    env:
      ARTIFACT_TAG: ${{ needs.preflight.outputs.tag }}
      # Free updater keys are available only to the existing trusted publication sources.
      MESH_TALK_UPDATER_PUBLIC_KEY: ${{ (needs.preflight.outputs.nightly == 'true' || startsWith(github.ref, 'refs/tags/v')) && vars.MESH_TALK_UPDATER_PUBLIC_KEY || '' }}
      TAURI_SIGNING_PRIVATE_KEY: ${{ (needs.preflight.outputs.nightly == 'true' || startsWith(github.ref, 'refs/tags/v')) && secrets.TAURI_SIGNING_PRIVATE_KEY || '' }}
      TAURI_SIGNING_PRIVATE_KEY_PASSWORD: ${{ (needs.preflight.outputs.nightly == 'true' || startsWith(github.ref, 'refs/tags/v')) && secrets.TAURI_SIGNING_PRIVATE_KEY_PASSWORD || '' }}
      MESH_TALK_BUILD_TAG: ${{ (needs.preflight.outputs.nightly == 'true' || startsWith(github.ref, 'refs/tags/')) && needs.preflight.outputs.tag || format('v{0}-dev', needs.preflight.outputs.version) }}
    strategy:
      fail-fast: false
      matrix:
        target:
          - x86_64-apple-darwin
          - aarch64-apple-darwin
          - x86_64-pc-windows-msvc
          - aarch64-pc-windows-msvc
          - x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu
          - aarch64-unknown-linux-gnu
        include:
          - os: macos
            runner: macos-latest
            target: x86_64-apple-darwin
          - os: macos
before publication builds. All three portable health platforms also execute the
bootstrap before merge, while actual signed six-platform artifacts and
downloaded draft verification remain required release evidence.

## Publish Nightly builds

The authoritative implementation is [the release workflow](../.github/workflows/release.yml),
with policy in `scripts/release/preflight.mjs`, `release-state.mjs`, and the existing artifact
verifiers. Stable and Nightly reuse the same platform matrix and verification owners.

- The daily schedule runs at **03:23 UTC** on `main`. It skips a source SHA already represented
  by a published Nightly. A manual **Tauri Release → Run workflow** on `main` with
  **publish_nightly** enabled requests a Nightly immediately. Leave **publish_release** disabled.
- Ordinary branch dispatches without either publication flag still produce private workflow
  artifacts only. Nightly publication rejects tag refs, other branches and simultaneous Stable
  publication requests. Only trusted main-branch code may use publication credentials.
- Preflight requires the existing exact-source CI/health/E2E/security checks before expensive
  Nightly builds. Missing or pending required checks are polled once per minute for up to four
  hours on the selected source SHA, so CI finishing after the scheduled start can proceed
  automatically. Completed unsuccessful checks, open scanning alerts and API errors fail
  immediately; a timeout reports the checks still pending. Retry after resolving those failures.
  Source evidence must come from the latest same-repository `push` run on `main` for each
  owning workflow at that exact SHA. PR, merge-queue, tag, scheduled and manual runs cannot
  substitute, even when their check names and SHA match. Each accepted check must belong
  to its owning workflow's selected check suite, and the latest run attempt must complete
  successfully; a newer pending or failed main run cannot fall back to older success.
  Successful jobs retained by a successful failed-jobs-only rerun remain valid in that suite.
  Final publication rechecks immediately and never accepts pending checks. Required checks and
  bounded waiting are owned by `scripts/release/check-source-checks.mjs`.
- CI generates `v<workspace-version>-nightly.<run-id>.<attempt>` and binds that technical tag to
  the exact source SHA. There is no manual version bump or official Stable tag. Never reuse or
  move a published Nightly tag, and never replace its uploaded assets.
- All desktop packages and post-office archives are built, signed and source-attested.
  The workflow verifies the downloaded workflow assets, creates a prerelease draft, uploads the
  complete set, re-downloads and verifies the draft, records asset IDs/digests, and publishes
  only that verified snapshot with `prerelease=true` and `make_latest=false`.

